<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Accessory Transit Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1851–1856</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Accessory Transit Company was an American firm founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornelius-vanderbilt">Cornelius Vanderbilt</a> that from 1851 carried travellers bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> across Nicaragua, by steamboat up the San Juan River and across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nicaragua">Lake Nicaragua</a>, then by stagecoach to the Pacific port of San Juan del Sur. The route was shorter than the crossing at Panama and carried tens of thousands of passengers. In 1856 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a> revoked its charter, and the route never fully recovered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/accessory-transit-company.jpg" alt="Accessory Transit Company" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unbekannte Autoren und Grafiker; Scan vom EDHAC e.V. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Accessory_Transit_Company_1855.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
